//! t24-red: gameplay shared between `cargo run` and the
//! editor's Play button.
//!
//! Scene content (entities, components, positions) lives in
//! `assets/*.bsn` files authored in the editor. Game behaviour
//! (systems, observers, resources) lives in [`GamePlugin`].
//!
//! # Try it
//!
//! 1. Open this project in jackdaw.
//! 2. Outliner: click `Add Entity` and pick `Cube`.
//! 3. Components panel: click `Add Component` and pick `SpinningCube`.
//!    Set `speed` to `1.5`.
//! 4. `File > Save`, then click Play: the menu comes up first — click
//!    **Start** and the cube spins in the level.
//! 5. `cargo run` launches the same game without the editor.
//!
//! # Adding your own components
//!
//! Write a component anywhere in this library (any module, not
//! `main.rs`), deriving `Component, Reflect, Default` with
//! `#[reflect(Component, Default)]`, like `SpinningCube` below. After you
//! save, click Rebuild in jackdaw (or run `jd build`) and it
//! appears in `Add Component`. No registration code is needed.
//!
//! # Tuning parameters (game-wide, saved as an asset)
//!
//! `GameConfig` below is the other half of the pattern: a *Resource*
//! whole systems read (`falling_cubes` uses `gravity` and
//! `move_speed`) whose value comes from an asset file,
//! `assets/game-config.bsn`. To change the tuning:
//!
//! 1. In jackdaw, `Project window > New Asset...` and pick
//!    `GameConfig` (after one Rebuild) — the editor creates
//!    `game-config.bsn` and opens its card.
//! 2. Edit `move_speed` (say `2.5`), `Save`.
//! 3. Play: the falling cube falls and spins by the file's numbers.
//!    `cargo run` and an exported build read the same file.
//!
//! Until that file exists the game runs on the `Default` values — a
//! missing config is not an error. Per-entity numbers (one cube's
//! `speed`) stay as components; game-wide numbers (gravity, move
//! speed, player health) belong in a config like this.
//!
//! # Input actions
//!
//! The same pattern drives input: `InputActionsPlugin` evaluates the
//! built-in actions (Move / Look / Jump / Interact / Pause — WASD and
//! the left stick, mouse and the right stick, Space / South, E / East,
//! Escape / Start) into the `GameActions` resource every frame; systems
//! read named actions instead of polling raw keys. To rebind, author
//! `assets/input-actions.bsn` the same way as the tuning config (the
//! plugin below wires the file; without it, the built-in table runs).
//!
//! In the editor's Play, click the game image to capture input and
//! Shift+Esc to give it back — a plain Esc is forwarded to the game
//! (that is why Pause can be Escape).
//!
//! # Physics & triggers
//!
//! The physics module (on by default) adds avian: things fall, land
//! and stay on colliders. Three pieces worth knowing:
//!
//! * **Colliders**: add an `AvianCollider` component to any entity
//!   (the editor's physics category) and pick a shape. A collider
//!   with no rigid body stands still as a wall or floor; a
//!   `RigidBody: Dynamic` one falls and lands — never through a
//!   floor. The falling cube and the pickups below are the examples.
//! * **Triggers**: an entity that is a collider *and* carries
//!   `Sensor` detects overlaps without blocking anything (the editor
//!   draws sensors with a cyan outline). Each frame,
//!   `TriggerEvents` lists every sensor that started or stopped
//!   touching something: `enters()` for pickup-style "got it",
//!   `exits()` for "left the area". The plugin wires the
//!   `CollisionEventsEnabled` prerequisite avian asks for — author
//!   the `Sensor`, nothing else. `collect_pickups` below is the
//!   whole pattern: the player walks into a pickup, it despawns and
//!   the count goes up — only the player's touch counts, so a cube
//!   settling onto a pickup leaves it alone; collect all three and
//!   the run is won — the flow moves to the game-over screen, the
//!   same move that ends a lost run, so the end screen's Restart
//!   starts the next one.
//! * **Collision layers**: a `CollisionLayers` component (fields
//!   `memberships` and `filters`, one bit per layer) decides who
//!   interacts with whom — a contact only exists when each side's
//!   memberships meet a layer the other side filters in. A common
//!   table: layer 1 the player and the world, layer 2 pickups,
//!   layer 3 hazards. Give hazards `memberships 0b100,
//!   filters 0b001` (hazard hits player only) and the player
//!   `0b001, 0b111` (hits everything) — pickups and hazards never
//!   interact with each other, so a drifting hazard cannot collect
//!   the pickups for the player. `spawn_hazards` below authors
//!   exactly that hazard row; the template player needs nothing
//!   (an entity without the component is on layer 1 with every
//!   filter, which is the table's player row already).
//!
//! The triggers feed more than pickups: `collect_pickups` counts and
//! `hazard_damage` burns health — same shape, different marker and
//! different reaction, and both read the one `TriggerEvents` the
//! bridge fills.
//!
//! Two gravities exist and they are different things:
//! `GameConfig.gravity` (the config asset above) is the author's
//! number, and at startup `apply_gravity_from_config` copies it
//! into the physics engine's `Gravity` resource — the one the
//! simulation actually uses. Editing the config file changes the
//! next launch; changing the `Gravity` resource in the editor's
//! Resources panel changes it for the running session.
//!
//! # Player controller
//!
//! The player the template spawns at (-2, 1, 0) is a real, if
//! minimal, character controller:
//!
//! * **Tuning is a component.** `PlayerController { move_speed,
//!   jump_speed }` lives on the player entity: select it and edit
//!   the numbers in the Components panel (per-entity numbers are
//!   components; game-wide numbers are the config above — the same
//!   split as one cube's `speed` vs the config's `move_speed`).
//! * **Movement is camera-relative.** W walks away from the camera
//!   wherever it looks; the body turns smoothly to face the way it
//!   moves. The one source of truth for "which way is forward" is
//!   the camera's `CameraLook.yaw`.
//! * **The camera is a child.** A `CameraRig` parented under the
//!   player is the whole follow-camera setup: `JackdawCameraRigPlugin`
//!   (wired in `GamePlugin` below) activates a lone rig by itself and
//!   drives it around its parent every frame. Author a second rig
//!   only together with an `ActiveCameraRig` component — with two
//!   rigs none self-activates; the game must choose.
//! * **Look has two channels that agree.** The `Look` action writes
//!   the rig's `CameraLook` every frame (`look_into_camera` below);
//!   while the right mouse button is held the rig's own mouse-look
//!   owns the turn instead — the bridge yields those frames so the
//!   two never double-write.
//! * **Jump and collision come from the physics module** (on by
//!   default): gravity — the very same `GameConfig.gravity` number
//!   the engine got — integrates onto the player's velocity, a short
//!   downward cast says when the feet are on the ground, and avian's
//!   `move_and_slide` sweeps the capsule so the player slides along
//!   walls instead of through them (walk into the stage walls to see
//!   it: head-on you stop, diagonally you slide). Without the physics
//!   module the player still walks, camera-relative and
//!   frame-rate-independent, but does not jump or collide.
//! * **A hand-placed player needs the full set.** `move_player`
//!   queries five components together, so an entity authored in the
//!   editor only moves when it carries `PlayerMarker`,
//!   `PlayerController`, a zero `PlayerVelocity`, a Kinematic
//!   `RigidBody`, and an `AvianCollider` (a capsule of radius 0.35
//!   and height 1.0 matches the template's). Parent a `CameraRig`
//!   under it and the game plays your level.
//!
//! # Game flow
//!
//! A game is more than one world: a menu to start from, the level to
//! play, an end screen when the run is over. The template ships all
//! three as real scene documents — `assets/menu.bsn`, `assets/scene.bsn`,
//! `assets/gameover.bsn` — and only one of them is in the world at a
//! time:
//!
//! * **States drive the worlds.** `GameFlow` below is a `States` enum
//!   (`Menu` / `Level` / `GameOver`). Each state's `OnEnter` shows its
//!   document by re-binding one scene root's handle — the runtime's
//!   whole switch primitive is that one insert, and the old scene's
//!   entities (its UI roots included) are gone before the new document
//!   spawns. Nothing accumulates across a menu → level → game over →
//!   menu loop.
//! * **Where a run begins is data.** `assets/game.flow.json` names the
//!   scene a standalone run starts from (`start_scene`) and lists the
//!   scenes that make up the game. The game boots into whatever it
//!   names: the menu document as the menu, the game-over document as
//!   the end screen, any other scene as a world to play. A broken or
//!   mis-pointed file warns and falls back — it never refuses to start.
//!   The editor validates the file, the Project window's right-click
//!   **Set as Startup Scene** writes it, and an export ships it.
//! * **What survives a switch is decided on purpose.** The player and
//!   everything your code spawns (health, counters, resources) belong
//!   to no scene document, so a switch leaves them alone — the player's
//!   entity id is one and the same from Start to Restart. Scene content
//!   (pickups, hazards, the hud) belongs to its world and dies with it.
//!   What crosses gets put back in shape instead: `reset_run` runs on
//!   every entry into the level, so Restart is just a switch to `Level`.
//! * **Each world brings its own camera.** The level's camera is the
//!   rig under the player; the menu and game-over documents get a
//!   state-scoped `Camera2d` (`DespawnOnExit` removes it with the
//!   state) — a UI document shown alone has nothing to render it
//!   otherwise, and the screen stays empty.
//! * **The editor's two Play buttons stay distinct.** Play runs the
//!   game from its flow-configured start scene; the scene-tab Play
//!   swaps the editor's own scene in as the world the game boots into,
//!   and the flow state follows it (that boot is the level).
//!
//! # Game audio
//!
//! Three sounds ship with the template, so audio works before you have
//! added a single file of your own: the menu's BGM (`menu.bsn`'s
//! `MenuBgm` entity), a click each screen button acknowledges itself with
//! (`press_sound` below), and the level's spatial drone (`scene.bsn`'s
//! `Ambient`, panned against the `SpatialListener` the player's camera
//! carries). Each is one entity carrying a `bevy_audio` component pair:
//! an `AudioPlayer("sounds/…")` naming a file and a `PlaybackSettings`
//! choosing loop/one-shot, volume and (for the drone) `spatial`. Note
//! bevy's spatial audio is left-right panning by distance, not HRTF.
//! The blip pulls double duty as the win's fanfare: `collect_pickups`
//! sounds it once on the sfx bus as the third pickup lands, right
//! before the flow moves to the end screen.
//!
//! The three-way mix — master, music, sfx — is the same config-asset
//! pattern as the tuning above: `GameAudioConfig` below, authored as
//! `assets/audio-config.bsn` through New Asset, with `MusicBus` /
//! `SfxBus` markers deciding which bus an entity rides. The engine only
//! reads `GlobalVolume` when it creates a sink and never follows a
//! change, so the template's `apply_audio_mix` builds each sink's real
//! volume as `base × bus × master` and re-applies it when the config
//! changes — a mid-run edit reaches sounds that are already playing.
//! While a sound plays, its entity grows a `SoundLive` component (the
//! `mirror_sound_live` system writes the sink's live numbers into it
//! every frame), which is what the Resources panel and a BRP query can
//! read — sinks themselves are not reflected.
//!
//! # Game UI
//!
//! `assets/hud.bsn` is a UI scene: the same kind of document as
//! `scene.bsn`, authored on the editor's 2D canvas (`Add` menu, `UI`
//! group — panels, labels, buttons, progress bars) with each widget's
//! `Bindings` component wiring it to the game. It is the level's own
//! overlay — the template spawns it when the level is current and takes
//! it away when the level ends — and its two screens show and hide
//! themselves:
//!
//! * **Screens follow state.** The pause screen's `Visibility` is bound
//!   to `GameState.paused` (the resource below). Flipping the bool is
//!   the whole mechanism — overlays inside one world are data, not
//!   switches; the worlds themselves are the flow above.
//! * **Buttons say which one was pressed.** Every button carries an
//!   `Action` binding that sends `ScreenPressed` with `button` set to
//!   its literal (`"resume"` here; the menu and game-over documents
//!   send `"start"`, `"restart"` and `"menu"`); the
//!   `on_screen_pressed` observer turns those into flow moves. The
//!   Pause *action* (Escape) lands on the same bool through
//!   `handle_pause_action`, so the key and the button agree.
//! * **The bar and the texts read the player.** The health bar's
//!   `Progress.value` is bound to `PlayerHealth.current` and `.max`
//!   through the registered `ratio` function, and the texts are
//!   `Text` bindings on the same components. Bindings can only call
//!   functions the game registers — that is what the
//!   `register_function` lines below are for. `wire_hud` is the
//!   other half: it puts a `BindContext(player)` on the document's
//!   `HudRoot` once it spawns, which is what `PlayerHealth...`
//!   resolves against.
//!
//! Edit `hud.bsn` in the editor, `Save`, and the running game picks
//! it up like `scene.bsn`. Layout discipline for your own screens:
//! percent and flex for anything position-like (the pause overlay
//! stretches to 100% of the window, the HUD hugs the top-left corner),
//! pixels only for sizes and padding — that is what keeps the screens
//! correct from 800x600 up to 2560x1440. One editor edge to know:
//! Play with a UI scene tab focused swaps the 3D scene for that UI
//! alone (it previews the canvas); play from the 3D scene tab to run
//! the game.
//!
//! # Saving progress and settings
//!
//! A run ends, the player drags a volume slider, and none of it is
//! lost: the template persists a small player profile — `GameSave`
//! below — through the runtime's `SavePlugin`, in one line:
//!
//! ```ignore
//! app.add_plugins(SavePlugin::<GameSave>::new(env!("CARGO_PKG_NAME")));
//! ```
//!
//! The file is `save.json` under the user's own data directory
//! (`~/Library/Application Support/<game-id>/` on macOS) — which is
//! what "separate from the scene files" means: scenes and configs are
//! project assets, the save belongs to the player, and an exported
//! build neither carries it nor overwrites it.
//!
//! The save is a second layer over the configs above: a config asset
//! is the authored default the game ships with, the save is what the
//! player changed. `apply_saved_audio` and `apply_saved_input` wait
//! until the authored configs have landed, then put the saved values
//! back over them; the `save_*_changes` watchers beside them persist
//! every later change — a slider drag, a BRP edit — the same frame,
//! so there is no save call to forget. A run ends at the game-over
//! screen and `count_run_over` counts it; the third run unlocks
//! `"endless"`; the menu's `Runs:` line reads a mirror of the save in
//! `GameState` (bindings resolve resources, and the save itself is
//! plain JSON you can open and read).
//!
//! The store guards itself: a missing file is a quiet first run, a
//! damaged one is renamed to `save.json.corrupt-<millis>` and the
//! game carries on, and a save from another version is never
//! overwritten — the runtime's `SaveStore` docs hold the details.


use bevy::audio::Volume;
use bevy::feathers::controls::FeathersSlider;
use bevy::prelude::*;
use bevy::scene::bsn;
use bevy::ui_widgets::{SliderValue, ValueChange};
use jackdaw_runtime::prelude::*;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

/// Your game's Bevy plugin. `main.rs` adds this for both `cargo run`
/// and the editor's Play button (same binary).
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct GamePlugin;

impl Plugin for GamePlugin {
    fn build(&self, app: &mut App) {
        // The boot scene, read once: the editor's Play override, then
        // the project's `game.flow.json`, then the template's menu. The
        // same string maps to the initial `GameFlow` state below and is
        // loaded by `spawn_scene`, so the state machine and the first
        // document can never disagree.
        let start = jackdaw_runtime::pie_scene_or(&jackdaw_runtime::flow::start_scene_or(
            MENU_SCENE,
        ));
        app.insert_resource(BootScene(start.clone()))
            .init_state::<GameFlow>()
            // Inserting the boot state runs its `OnEnter` on the first
            // frame — the game is born in the right world, not switched
            // into it later.
            .insert_state(boot_state(&start))
            // `spawn_hud` in the Update watcher, never before the main
            // root: the editor's Play swaps whichever scene root landed
            // at the LOWEST entity index, and a hud root below the game
            // root means Play swaps the hud document instead of the
            // world (T12-T2 caught this live; the flow re-spawns the hud
            // per level entry, so the ordering is pinned here every
            // time, not just at startup).
            .add_systems(Startup, spawn_scene)
            .add_systems(Update, spin_cubes)
            // One line per config type: register it as an asset (so the
            // editor offers it in New Asset) and load `game-config.bsn`
            // as the starting value of the `GameConfig` resource.
            .init_asset::<GameConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameConfig>()
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameConfig>::default())
            // Audio (see "Game audio" above): the three-way mix config, the
            // systems that build and refresh each sink's real volume, and
            // the mirror that makes playing sounds inspectable.
            .init_asset::<GameAudioConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameAudioConfig>()
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameAudioConfig>::new("audio-config.bsn"))
            // `AudioPlayer` is generic, and the reflect derive's automatic
            // registration skips generic types: without this one line the
            // scene loader cannot name the component a scene authored.
            .register_type::<AudioPlayer<AudioSource>>()
            // The pause screen's sliders emit this; generic for the same
            // reason, so a BRP client can fire it by name (L leg discipline:
            // drive the real event, not a stand-in).
            .register_type::<ValueChange<f32>>()
            // The sliders themselves attach from code (`attach_volume_sliders`
            // below); feathers types are not in the reflect auto-inventory,
            // so this line is what lets a BRP client name the widget.
            .register_type::<FeathersSlider>()
            // The slider's bar is a `BackgroundGradient` on the widget
            // root, and a scene component's pieces apply through the type
            // registry: a type the registry cannot name is dropped from
            // the widget (its bar then never paints). These are the
            // gradient's face and payload.
            .register_type::<bevy::ui::BackgroundGradient>()
            .register_type::<bevy::ui::Gradient>()
            .register_type::<bevy::ui::ColorStop>()
            .register_type::<bevy::ui::InterpolationColorSpace>()
            // The scene gives the slider its bones (track, thumb, digits);
            // the feathers runtime dresses them: theme observers, the
            // embedded fonts the digits read, and the control style systems
            // that fill the bar and move the thumb with the value. Without
            // it the widget spawns and works but draws nothing. Coexists
            // with the widgets runtime's own theme (its finish runs last
            // by design).
            .add_plugins(bevy::feathers::FeathersPlugins)
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                // Chained: a fresh sink gets the mix before the mirror reads
                // it — tuple order alone is not execution order.
                (apply_audio_mix, apply_audio_mix_to_new_sinks, mirror_sound_live).chain(),
            )
            // Saving (see "Saving progress and settings" above): the
            // player's profile in `save.json` under the user data
            // directory. The plugin loads the file, inserts the store
            // and registers it for reflection; the systems below are
            // the whole integration.
            .add_plugins(SavePlugin::<GameSave>::new(env!("CARGO_PKG_NAME")))
            .add_observer(on_volume_changed)
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                // The saved values stand over the authored defaults: each
                // system waits until the authored config has landed (its
                // loader despawned — that is the readiness gate both check),
                // then the bridge applies the save over it once, and the
                // watcher arms on the landing frame — skipping it, so an
                // authored injection is never persisted as a player change.
                // Every change after that frame (a slider drag, a BRP edit)
                // lands in the save the same frame.
                (apply_saved_audio, save_audio_changes)
                    .after(jackdaw_runtime::apply_config_defaults::<GameAudioConfig>),
            )
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                (apply_saved_input, save_input_changes)
                    .after(jackdaw_runtime::apply_config_defaults::<GameInputConfig>),
            )
            .add_systems(Update, (attach_volume_sliders, sync_volume_sliders, mirror_save_display))
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), spawn_falling_cube)
            .add_systems(Update, falling_cubes)
            // Input: the built-in five actions evaluate every frame; the
            // `input-actions.bsn` asset (same New Asset flow) overrides
            // bindings and deadzones when the game registers the type.
            .init_asset::<GameInputConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameInputConfig>()
            .add_plugins(InputActionsPlugin)
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameInputConfig>::new(
                "input-actions.bsn",
            ))
            // The player spawns once — on the first entry into the level
            // — and then crosses every switch untouched (see
            // `spawn_player`; `reset_run` puts its numbers back).
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), (spawn_player, reset_run).chain())
            .add_systems(Update, (move_player, look_into_camera))
            // The camera rig driver: activates the rig under the player
            // and follows it every frame (see "Player controller" above).
            .add_plugins(JackdawCameraRigPlugin)
            // Game flow (see "Game flow" above): the per-state document
            // swaps and cameras, the level-scoped hud, the observer the
            // flow documents' buttons feed, and the systems that move
            // the state. Types (`GameFlow`, `GameState`, `PlayerHealth`,
            // `ScreenPressed`) need no registration — the `Reflect`
            // derives announce them the same way they reach the editor's
            // Add Component list; the via functions are the one binding
            // piece a game registers by hand.
            .init_resource::<GameState>()
            .register_function(ratio)
            .register_function(is_zero)
            .register_function(not)
            .add_observer(on_screen_pressed)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Menu), enter_menu)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), enter_level)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::GameOver), (enter_game_over, count_run_over))
            .add_systems(Update, spawn_hud_while_level)
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                // Chained: the player spawns with a zeroed `PlayerHealth`,
                // and an `update_game_over` that ran first would read
                // current <= 0 on frame one and boot the game straight
                // into game over (T12-T2 caught this live in PIE).
                (wire_hud, (apply_health_from_config, update_game_over).chain()),
            )
            // The pause flip owns the frame: a Pause press never also
            // moves the player, whichever way the bool lands. Registered
            // HERE and only here — adding it twice (tuple + edge) runs
            // the toggle twice per press and Escape never pauses
            // (T12-T2 caught this live).
            .add_systems(Update, handle_pause_action.before(move_player));
        // Physics & triggers (the docs at the top walk the pattern):
        // the bridge turns sensor contacts into `TriggerEvents` —
        // `collect_pickups` and `hazard_damage` read it — and
        // `apply_gravity_from_config` hands the config's gravity
        // number to the engine. The props are level scenery:
        // spawned on entry, gone on exit, back fresh on the next
        // one.
        app.add_plugins(jackdaw_avian_integration::TriggerPlugin)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), reset_run_counters)
            .add_systems(
                OnEnter(GameFlow::Level),
                (spawn_stage, spawn_pickups, spawn_walls, spawn_hazards),
            )
            .add_systems(Update, (collect_pickups, hazard_damage, apply_gravity_from_config));
    }
}

/// Spawn the one scene root the flow re-binds: `JackdawSceneRoot` is
/// the runtime's switch primitive, and re-inserting it with a new
/// handle is a whole world swap (the old document's entities despawn,
/// the new one spawns when it loads). The handle is the boot scene —
/// `game.flow.json`'s `start_scene` unless the editor's Play put one of
/// its own there — the same string `boot_state` mapped the initial
/// state from. One root for all three worlds: the flow moves the
/// document under it, and the root itself (and this marker) is the
/// stable thing across every switch.
fn spawn_scene(
    mut commands: Commands,
    boot: Res<BootScene>,
    asset_server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    commands.spawn((
        Name::new("GameRoot"),
        GameRoot,
        JackdawSceneRoot(asset_server.load(boot.0.clone())),
    ));
}

/// Spin-rate in radians per second. Attach it in the Components panel; the
/// editor sees this component with no registration code because Bevy's
/// `reflect_auto_register` picks up the `Reflect` derive. `Default` +
/// `#[reflect(Default)]` let the editor add it and the game reconstruct
/// it with sensible starting values.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct SpinningCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spin_cubes(time: Res<Time>, mut cubes: Query<(&SpinningCube, &mut Transform)>) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
        transform.rotate_y(cube.speed * dt);
    }
}

/// Game-wide tuning parameters. Both an `Asset` (the editor authors
/// `game-config.bsn` files of it) and a `Resource` (systems read it),
/// so the value can be edited in the editor, saved to disk, and loaded
/// back as the starting value in every run — see "Tuning parameters"
/// above. `player_health` is the run's starting health:
/// `apply_health_from_config` hands it to the player's `PlayerHealth`
/// component when the file loads — the config is the author's intent,
/// the component is the live state after that.
#[derive(Asset, Resource, Reflect, Clone)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Game Config"))]
pub struct GameConfig {
    /// The health a run starts at, handed to the player's
    /// `PlayerHealth` (see "Game UI" above).
    pub player_health: f32,
    /// Multiplies how fast things move and spin.
    pub move_speed: f32,
    /// Downward acceleration, in units per second squared.
    pub gravity: f32,
}

impl Default for GameConfig {
    /// Sensible numbers a game runs on before anyone has authored
    /// `game-config.bsn` — a missing config is not an error.
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            player_health: 100.0,
            move_speed: 1.0,
            gravity: -9.81,
        }
    }
}

// ---- Game audio: the three-way mix and the live mirror ----------------

/// Game-wide audio levels, the config-asset pattern again: author
/// `assets/audio-config.bsn` through New Asset (or edit the file), and
/// `apply_audio_mix` rebuilds every sink's real volume from it — live, in
/// the editor's Play and in an exported build alike. Until the file exists
/// the game runs on the `Default` (everything full).
#[derive(Asset, Resource, Reflect, Clone)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Game Config"))]
pub struct GameAudioConfig {
    /// Multiplies every sound in the game.
    pub master: f32,
    /// Multiplies every sound whose entity carries a `MusicBus`.
    pub music: f32,
    /// Multiplies every sound whose entity carries an `SfxBus`.
    pub sfx: f32,
}

impl Default for GameAudioConfig {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            master: 1.0,
            music: 1.0,
            sfx: 1.0,
        }
    }
}

/// Routes an entity's sound onto the music bus. A component the editor can
/// add and a scene can author; `apply_audio_mix` reads it as a factor of
/// `GameAudioConfig.music`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Audio"))]
pub struct MusicBus;

/// Routes an entity's sound onto the sfx bus, same shape as [`MusicBus`].
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Audio"))]
pub struct SfxBus;

/// What one sink actually plays at, right now. bevy's `AudioSink` and
/// `SpatialAudioSink` are not reflected, so nothing outside the process can
/// read them; this mirror is the inspectable surface — `mirror_sound_live`
/// writes the sink's live numbers into it every frame, and the Resources
/// panel (or a BRP query) reads it like any component.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Clone, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Audio"))]
pub struct SoundLive {
    /// Which bus the sound rides: `"music"`, `"sfx"`, or `"plain"`.
    pub kind: String,
    /// The mixed volume the sink plays at (linear scale).
    pub volume: f32,
    /// Whether the sink is paused.
    pub paused: bool,
    /// Whether the sink has nothing left to play (a one-shot that ended).
    pub finished: bool,
    /// How far into the clip the sink is, in seconds.
    pub position_secs: f32,
}

/// A sink's real volume: its authored base times its bus times the master.
/// The engine reads `GlobalVolume` exactly once, when it creates the sink,
/// and never follows a change — which is why the mix lives here rather
/// than in `GlobalVolume`.
fn mix_volume(base: f32, music: bool, sfx: bool, config: &GameAudioConfig) -> f32 {
    let bus = if music {
        config.music
    } else if sfx {
        config.sfx
    } else {
        1.0
    };
    base * bus * config.master
}

/// Re-apply the mix to every sink when the config changes — this is the
/// half the engine leaves undone ("Changing GlobalVolume does not affect
/// already playing audio"): a mid-run edit reaches sounds that are
/// already playing. Runs as a no-op unless the resource changed.
fn apply_audio_mix(
    config: Res<GameAudioConfig>,
    plain: Query<
        (
            &PlaybackSettings,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
            &mut AudioSink,
        ),
        Without<SpatialAudioSink>,
    >,
    spatial: Query<
        (
            &PlaybackSettings,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
            &mut SpatialAudioSink,
        ),
        Without<AudioSink>,
    >,
) {
    if !config.is_changed() {
        return;
    }
    for (settings, music, sfx, mut sink) in plain {
        let volume = mix_volume(
            settings.volume.to_linear(),
            music.is_some(),
            sfx.is_some(),
            &config,
        );
        sink.set_volume(Volume::Linear(volume));
    }
    for (settings, music, sfx, mut sink) in spatial {
        let volume = mix_volume(
            settings.volume.to_linear(),
            music.is_some(),
            sfx.is_some(),
            &config,
        );
        sink.set_volume(Volume::Linear(volume));
    }
}

/// Write the mix onto each sink the frame it appears: the engine built it
/// from the authored base alone (times `GlobalVolume`, which the template
/// does not use), so a new sink on a bus starts at the wrong level until
/// this runs.
fn apply_audio_mix_to_new_sinks(
    config: Res<GameAudioConfig>,
    plain: Query<
        (
            &PlaybackSettings,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
            &mut AudioSink,
        ),
        (Added<AudioSink>, Without<SpatialAudioSink>),
    >,
    spatial: Query<
        (
            &PlaybackSettings,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
            &mut SpatialAudioSink,
        ),
        Added<SpatialAudioSink>,
    >,
) {
    for (settings, music, sfx, mut sink) in plain {
        let volume = mix_volume(
            settings.volume.to_linear(),
            music.is_some(),
            sfx.is_some(),
            &config,
        );
        sink.set_volume(Volume::Linear(volume));
    }
    for (settings, music, sfx, mut sink) in spatial {
        let volume = mix_volume(
            settings.volume.to_linear(),
            music.is_some(),
            sfx.is_some(),
            &config,
        );
        sink.set_volume(Volume::Linear(volume));
    }
}

/// Keep [`SoundLive`] true. Every entity playing a sound grows one (this
/// system inserts it the first frame it sees the sink), and from then on it
/// carries the sink's live numbers — the one reflected surface for audio a
/// game, the editor or a BRP query can read.
fn mirror_sound_live(
    mut commands: Commands,
    plain: Query<
        (
            Entity,
            &AudioSink,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
        ),
        Without<SpatialAudioSink>,
    >,
    spatial: Query<
        (
            Entity,
            &SpatialAudioSink,
            Option<&MusicBus>,
            Option<&SfxBus>,
        ),
        Without<AudioSink>,
    >,
    mut live: Query<&mut SoundLive>,
) {
    let mut update =
        |entity: Entity,
         kind: &str,
         volume: f32,
         paused: bool,
         finished: bool,
         position: f32| {
            let value = SoundLive {
                kind: kind.to_string(),
                volume,
                paused,
                finished,
                position_secs: position,
            };
            match live.get_mut(entity) {
                Ok(mut slot) => *slot = value,
                Err(_) => {
                    commands.entity(entity).insert(value);
                }
            }
        };
    for (entity, sink, music, sfx) in plain {
        let kind = if music.is_some() {
            "music"
        } else if sfx.is_some() {
            "sfx"
        } else {
            "plain"
        };
        update(
            entity,
            kind,
            sink.volume().to_linear(),
            sink.is_paused(),
            sink.empty(),
            sink.position().as_secs_f32(),
        );
    }
    for (entity, sink, music, sfx) in spatial {
        let kind = if music.is_some() {
            "music"
        } else if sfx.is_some() {
            "sfx"
        } else {
            "plain"
        };
        update(
            entity,
            kind,
            sink.volume().to_linear(),
            sink.is_paused(),
            sink.empty(),
            sink.position().as_secs_f32(),
        );
    }
}

// ---- Game save: the player's layer ------------------------------------
// The persisted half of progress, unlocks and settings. The runtime's
// `SaveStore` owns the file, the version guard and the three-state
// fallback; the systems below are the whole game-side integration: two
// bridges that restore the saved settings over the authored defaults,
// two watchers that persist the player's changes, the run counter, and
// the pause screen's volume sliders (attached from code, see
// `attach_volume_sliders`).

/// What this game saves. Plain serde JSON inside the runtime's
/// envelope (`save.json` under the user data directory). Extend it
/// freely: the runtime versions the envelope, and a save whose
/// `profile` no longer fits this type is treated like a damaged one —
/// backed up, defaults in, the game runs.
#[derive(Reflect, Clone, Default, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[reflect(Default)]
pub struct GameSave {
    /// How many runs have ended (a run ends at the game-over screen).
    pub progress: GameProgress,
    /// Things the player has unlocked; `"endless"` lands here on the
    /// third finished run (`count_run_over`).
    pub unlocks: Vec<String>,
    /// The player's audio mix, when they have changed it. `None` — the
    /// fresh first run — keeps the authored `audio-config.bsn` values
    /// (or the defaults).
    pub audio: Option<AudioPrefs>,
    /// The player's rebound actions, when they have changed them.
    /// Restored through the same union merge `input-actions.bsn` gets,
    /// so actions the save omits keep their authored bindings.
    pub input: Option<Vec<jackdaw_runtime::GameActionDef>>,
}

/// The run counter and whatever else a real game would add (score,
/// checkpoints). The template keeps the one number the menu shows.
#[derive(Reflect, Clone, Default, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[reflect(Default)]
pub struct GameProgress {
    /// Finished runs, counted at every game-over.
    pub runs_played: u32,
}

/// The player's audio mix as the save holds it — the same three knobs
/// `GameAudioConfig` authors, minus the asset plumbing.
#[derive(Reflect, Clone, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[reflect(Default)]
pub struct AudioPrefs {
    pub master: f32,
    pub music: f32,
    pub sfx: f32,
}

impl AudioPrefs {
    fn from_config(config: &GameAudioConfig) -> Self {
        Self {
            master: config.master,
            music: config.music,
            sfx: config.sfx,
        }
    }

    fn apply_to(&self, config: &mut GameAudioConfig) {
        config.master = self.master;
        config.music = self.music;
        config.sfx = self.sfx;
    }
}

/// How many finished runs unlock [`UNLOCK_ENDLESS`].
const UNLOCK_ENDLESS_AFTER: u32 = 3;

/// The unlock `count_run_over` grants: example data, a string in the
/// save's `unlocks` list.
const UNLOCK_ENDLESS: &str = "endless";

/// Put the saved mix over the authored one, once, after the authored
/// config has landed — a `ConfigLoader` still in flight means
/// `apply_config_defaults` may yet inject the asset's values and would
/// wash the save back out. No saved mix (`None`) is the common first
/// run: the authored values simply stand.
fn apply_saved_audio(
    loaders: Query<(), With<ConfigLoader<GameAudioConfig>>>,
    mut config: ResMut<GameAudioConfig>,
    save: Res<SaveStore<GameSave>>,
    mut armed: Local<bool>,
) {
    if *armed || !loaders.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    if let Some(prefs) = save.profile.audio.as_ref() {
        prefs.apply_to(&mut config);
    }
    *armed = true;
}

/// Persist the player's mix. Armed on the frame the authored config
/// lands — skipping that frame is what keeps the *authored* injection
/// from being persisted as a player change, and what keeps a first run
/// that never touched a slider from leaving a `save.json` behind at
/// all. Every change after that frame lands in the save the same frame
/// (`get_mut` marks the store dirty; the runtime's flush writes it).
fn save_audio_changes(
    config: Res<GameAudioConfig>,
    loaders: Query<(), With<ConfigLoader<GameAudioConfig>>>,
    mut save: ResMut<SaveStore<GameSave>>,
    mut armed: Local<bool>,
) {
    if !*armed {
        if loaders.is_empty() {
            *armed = true;
        }
        return;
    }
    if !config.is_changed() {
        return;
    }
    let prefs = save
        .get_mut()
        .audio
        .get_or_insert_with(AudioPrefs::default);
    *prefs = AudioPrefs::from_config(&config);
}

/// Put the saved rebinds over the authored table, once, after the
/// authored input config has landed — the same union merge
/// `input-actions.bsn` gets: saved entries win by name, everything the
/// save omits keeps its authored (or built-in) binding.
fn apply_saved_input(
    loaders: Query<(), With<ConfigLoader<GameInputConfig>>>,
    mut config: ResMut<GameInputConfig>,
    save: Res<SaveStore<GameSave>>,
    mut armed: Local<bool>,
) {
    if *armed || !loaders.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    if let Some(saved) = save.profile.input.as_ref() {
        config.actions = jackdaw_runtime::merge_actions(&config.actions, saved);
    }
    *armed = true;
}

/// Persist the player's action table. Armed like the audio watcher:
/// the authored injection frame is skipped, so a first run without any
/// rebind writes nothing.
fn save_input_changes(
    config: Res<GameInputConfig>,
    loaders: Query<(), With<ConfigLoader<GameInputConfig>>>,
    mut save: ResMut<SaveStore<GameSave>>,
    mut armed: Local<bool>,
) {
    if !*armed {
        if loaders.is_empty() {
            *armed = true;
        }
        return;
    }
    if !config.is_changed() {
        return;
    }
    save.get_mut().input = Some(config.actions.clone());
}

/// A run ends at the game-over screen: count it, and grant `"endless"`
/// on the third. `get_mut` marks the store dirty, and the runtime's
/// flush writes the file on the same frame — no save call to forget.
fn count_run_over(mut save: ResMut<SaveStore<GameSave>>) {
    let profile = save.get_mut();
    profile.progress.runs_played += 1;
    if profile.progress.runs_played >= UNLOCK_ENDLESS_AFTER
        && !profile
            .unlocks
            .iter()
            .any(|unlock| unlock == UNLOCK_ENDLESS)
    {
        profile.unlocks.push(UNLOCK_ENDLESS.to_string());
    }
}

/// The save's display mirror (see `GameState`): runs and the unlock
/// note copied across on every frame they differ. A restart lands here
/// too — the file loads before the first update, so the menu shows the
/// restored numbers at once.
fn mirror_save_display(save: Res<SaveStore<GameSave>>, mut state: ResMut<GameState>) {
    let runs = save.profile.progress.runs_played;
    let note = if save
        .profile
        .unlocks
        .iter()
        .any(|unlock| unlock == UNLOCK_ENDLESS)
    {
        "Endless mode unlocked!"
    } else {
        ""
    };
    if state.runs_played != runs {
        state.runs_played = runs;
    }
    if state.unlock_note != note {
        state.unlock_note = note.to_string();
    }
}

/// Marks the pause screen's master-volume row (authored in hud.bsn),
/// the attach point [`attach_volume_sliders`] hangs the slider on — and
/// (once the slider is attached) the marker the observer and the sync
/// find the widget by. Music and sfx repeat the shape.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct MasterVolumeSlider;

/// The music-volume slider's marker, same shape as [`MasterVolumeSlider`].
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct MusicVolumeSlider;

/// The sfx-volume slider's marker, same shape as [`MasterVolumeSlider`].
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct SfxVolumeSlider;

/// Attach the three sliders to the pause screen's authored rows. A
/// `FeathersSlider` is a *scene component*: the widget is a small scene
/// (track, thumb, digits) that only comes into the world through the
/// scene API — spawned as a bare component line in a document it would
/// sit dead, its scene never built. `spawn_scene` is the whole
/// attachment: the scene grows under the authored row, beside its
/// label, and the marker rides the widget so the observer and the sync
/// below keep pointing at it. `Added` re-fires for every fresh hud the
/// flow spawns, so each entry into the level re-attaches itself.
fn attach_volume_sliders(
    mut commands: Commands,
    // `Without<SliderValue>`: the marker rides the attached widget too (it
    // is how the observer and the sync know which knob spoke), and without
    // the exclusion the widget itself would re-match this query on the next
    // frame — each slider spawning another slider under it, forever.
    masters: Query<Entity, (Added<MasterVolumeSlider>, Without<SliderValue>)>,
    musics: Query<Entity, (Added<MusicVolumeSlider>, Without<SliderValue>)>,
    sfxs: Query<Entity, (Added<SfxVolumeSlider>, Without<SliderValue>)>,
) {
    for entity in &masters {
        commands
            .spawn_scene(bsn! { @FeathersSlider })
            .insert((MasterVolumeSlider, ChildOf(entity)));
    }
    for entity in &musics {
        commands
            .spawn_scene(bsn! { @FeathersSlider })
            .insert((MusicVolumeSlider, ChildOf(entity)));
    }
    for entity in &sfxs {
        commands
            .spawn_scene(bsn! { @FeathersSlider })
            .insert((SfxVolumeSlider, ChildOf(entity)));
    }
}

/// The pause screen's volume sliders write the mix: the widget emits
/// `ValueChange<f32>`, this observer tells which slider spoke (its
/// marker) and moves that knob of `GameAudioConfig` — the one resource
/// `apply_audio_mix` and the save watcher both read. An observer is
/// the whole bridge: a binding can read a resource but not write one.
fn on_volume_changed(
    change: On<ValueChange<f32>>,
    masters: Query<(), With<MasterVolumeSlider>>,
    musics: Query<(), With<MusicVolumeSlider>>,
    sfxs: Query<(), With<SfxVolumeSlider>>,
    mut config: ResMut<GameAudioConfig>,
) {
    if masters.contains(change.source) {
        config.master = change.value;
    } else if musics.contains(change.source) {
        config.music = change.value;
    } else if sfxs.contains(change.source) {
        config.sfx = change.value;
    }
}

/// Point the sliders back at the mix. The widget's value is
/// external-state it never reads from the game, so everything that
/// moves `GameAudioConfig` — the saved restore at startup, a BRP edit,
/// an authored change — lands in the slider position here. No change
/// detection gate on purpose: the sliders spawn when the level starts,
/// long after the startup restore moved the config, and a spawned
/// slider must pick that value up anyway. `SliderValue` is immutable,
/// so a sync is a re-insert; the equality guard keeps a drag from
/// feeding back on itself.
fn sync_volume_sliders(
    mut commands: Commands,
    config: Res<GameAudioConfig>,
    masters: Query<(Entity, &SliderValue), With<MasterVolumeSlider>>,
    musics: Query<(Entity, &SliderValue), With<MusicVolumeSlider>>,
    sfxs: Query<(Entity, &SliderValue), With<SfxVolumeSlider>>,
) {
    for (entity, value) in &masters {
        if (value.0 - config.master).abs() > 1e-5 {
            commands.entity(entity).insert(SliderValue(config.master));
        }
    }
    for (entity, value) in &musics {
        if (value.0 - config.music).abs() > 1e-5 {
            commands.entity(entity).insert(SliderValue(config.music));
        }
    }
    for (entity, value) in &sfxs {
        if (value.0 - config.sfx).abs() > 1e-5 {
            commands.entity(entity).insert(SliderValue(config.sfx));
        }
    }
}

/// A cube the config drives, spawned so there is something to watch:
/// it falls under gravity and spins at its component's `speed`
/// scaled by `GameConfig::move_speed` — the per-entity number and
/// the game-wide number working together.
///
/// With the physics module (the default) the fall is the engine's:
/// the cube is a dynamic body with its own collider, dropped onto
/// the stage `spawn_stage` lays below it, so it lands and stays.
/// The spin stays the game's own — a system, not a force.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct FallingCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spawn_falling_cube(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    // No `SpinningCube` here: two systems rotating one entity stack
    // their rates, which reads as the config having no effect. The
    // falling cube's spin belongs to `move_speed` alone, so one value
    // changes one behavior.
    // The cube is level scenery: spawned with the level, gone with it
    // (`DespawnOnExit`), back fresh the next time the level starts.
    commands.spawn((
        FallingCube { speed: 1.0 },
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(0.6, 0.6, 0.6))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.9, 0.9, 0.9),
            ..default()
        })),
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, 3.0, 0.0),
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
        avian3d::prelude::RigidBody::Dynamic,
        jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
            avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Cuboid {
                x_length: 0.6,
                y_length: 0.6,
                z_length: 0.6,
            },
        ),
    ));
}

fn falling_cubes(
    time: Res<Time>,
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut cubes: Query<(&FallingCube, &mut Transform)>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
        transform.rotate_y(cube.speed * config.move_speed * dt);
    }
}

/// Marks the entity the five input actions drive. The template spawns
/// one complete; the full set a hand-authored player needs is listed
/// under "Player controller" above (with physics: five components,
/// two without). Its camera is a rig parented under it — or an
/// `ActiveCameraRig` when another rig already exists.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerMarker;

/// The author's half of the player: the tuning the Components panel
/// edits and the scene saves. Per-entity numbers belong on the entity,
/// game-wide numbers in `GameConfig` — the same split as `SpinningCube`'s
/// `speed` against the config's `move_speed`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerController {
    /// Ground speed, metres per second.
    pub move_speed: f32,
    /// Launch speed of a jump, metres per second (straight up).
    pub jump_speed: f32,
}

impl Default for PlayerController {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            move_speed: 4.0,
            jump_speed: 4.5,
        }
    }
}

/// The player: spawned once, on the first entry into the level, and
/// never again. It is code-spawned and part of no scene document, so
/// every switch the flow makes leaves it alone — its entity id is one
/// and the same from Start through game over to Restart (the run's
/// container, not the run). What changes is its numbers, and
/// `reset_run` (right after this on every level entry) is what puts
/// them back.
fn spawn_player(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
    existing: Query<(), With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if !existing.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    // The visible body. The capsule matches the collider below, so what
    // you see is what collides. `PlayerHealth` is what the hud reads:
    // it starts at the `Default` and `apply_health_from_config` (below)
    // hands the config's `player_health` over as soon as it loads.
    let player = commands
        .spawn((
            PlayerMarker,
            PlayerController::default(),
            PlayerHealth::default(),
            Mesh3d(meshes.add(Capsule3d::new(0.35, 1.0))),
            MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
                base_color: Color::srgb(0.25, 0.55, 0.9),
                ..default()
            })),
            Transform::from_translation(PLAYER_SPAWN),
        avian3d::prelude::RigidBody::Kinematic,
        jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
            avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Capsule {
                radius: 0.35,
                height: 1.0,
            },
        ),
        PlayerVelocity(Vec3::ZERO),
        PickupCount(0),
        ))
        .id();
    // The follow camera: a rig parented to the player. Parenting is the
    // whole setup — the plugin above activates a lone rig by itself and
    // drives it around its parent every frame. The `SpatialListener` rides
    // the same entity: spatial sounds (like the level's `Ambient` drone)
    // pan and fade against wherever the player is.
    commands.spawn((SpatialListener::default(), CameraRig::default(), ChildOf(player)));
}

/// The horizontal direction the Move action asks for this frame, in
/// world space but relative to the camera: rotate the stick/WASD input
/// by the camera's yaw so W is always "away from the camera", then
/// clamp to unit length so diagonals are not faster.
fn move_direction(move_input: Vec2, camera_yaw: f32) -> Vec3 {
    let forward = Quat::from_rotation_y(camera_yaw) * -Vec3::Z;
    let right = Quat::from_rotation_y(camera_yaw) * Vec3::X;
    let mut direction = right * move_input.x + forward * move_input.y;
    direction.y = 0.0;
    if direction.length_squared() > 1.0 {
        direction = direction.normalize();
    }
    direction
}

/// How quickly the body turns to face where it walks: the fraction of
/// the turn to take scales with this, so the turn takes roughly a
/// tenth of a second at any frame rate.
const PLAYER_TURN_RATE: f32 = 12.0;

/// Turn `transform` to face `direction` smoothly — the body runs to
/// face where it walks instead of snapping. Skipped when there is no
/// movement this frame.
fn face_movement_direction(direction: Vec3, dt: f32, transform: &mut Transform) {
    let horizontal = Vec3::new(direction.x, 0.0, direction.z);
    if horizontal.length_squared() < 1e-6 {
        return;
    }
    let facing = horizontal.normalize();
    // `-Z` faces the rotation's "forward"; solve for the yaw that puts
    // it along `facing`.
    let target = Quat::from_rotation_y((-facing.x).atan2(-facing.z));
    let turn = (PLAYER_TURN_RATE * dt).clamp(0.0, 1.0);
    transform.rotation = transform.rotation.slerp(target, turn);
}

/// The Look action drives the rig's orientation: mouse (and right
/// stick) motion becomes `CameraLook` yaw and pitch — the same state
/// the rig's own right-mouse-drag channel writes. While that button is
/// held the bridge yields, so the two channels never double-write the
/// same turn.
fn look_into_camera(
    actions: Res<GameActions>,
    mouse: Option<Res<ButtonInput<MouseButton>>>,
    mut rigs: Query<(&CameraRig, &mut CameraLook)>,
) {
    if mouse
        .as_deref()
        .is_some_and(|buttons| buttons.pressed(MouseButton::Right))
    {
        return;
    }
    let look = actions.value("Look");
    if look == Vec2::ZERO {
        return;
    }
    for (rig, mut camera) in &mut rigs {
        camera.yaw -= look.x * rig.sensitivity;
        camera.pitch = match rig.mode {
            // Third person: look down increases the pitch, bounded to
            // stay above the horizon.
            CameraMode::ThirdPerson => {
                (camera.pitch + look.y * rig.sensitivity).clamp(0.05, 1.4)
            }
            // First person: look up increases the pitch, bounded to
            // stay short of straight up/down.
            CameraMode::FirstPerson => {
                (camera.pitch - look.y * rig.sensitivity).clamp(-1.4, 1.4)
            }
        };
    }
}

// ---- Game flow: three worlds, one state machine ----------------------
// The states, the document each one shows, the cameras that render the
// ones without a rig, and the systems that move between them. The
// documents themselves are data — open `menu.bsn`, `scene.bsn` or
// `gameover.bsn` in the editor to restyle or rewire them.

/// The scene documents the flow moves between, spelled once: the boot
/// fallback, the state mapping and the per-state swaps all name the
/// same three files.
const MENU_SCENE: &str = "menu.bsn";
const LEVEL_SCENE: &str = "scene.bsn";
const GAME_OVER_SCENE: &str = "gameover.bsn";
/// The level's overlay (see "Game UI" below) — not a world of its own,
/// so it is not in the flow file's rotation; it lives and dies with
/// `LEVEL_SCENE`.
const HUD_SCENE: &str = "hud.bsn";

/// The scene the game boots into, read once in `GamePlugin::build`:
/// the same string maps to the initial [`GameFlow`] state (through
/// [`boot_state`]) and is loaded by [`spawn_scene`], so the state
/// machine and the first document can never disagree (that mismatch is
/// exactly what a Play-Current launch would otherwise produce).
#[derive(Resource)]
struct BootScene(String);

/// Which world the game is in. The `States` derive makes it bevy's
/// state machine: `OnEnter`/`OnExit` schedules run per state and
/// `DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Menu)` removes an entity when `Menu` ends.
/// The worlds are mutually exclusive by construction — the flow re-binds
/// one scene root per state — and the hud's bindings deliberately do
/// not read this enum: bindings resolve resource and component paths,
/// so the one bool they do read lives in [`GameState`] next door.
/// States for worlds, bools for binding data.
#[derive(States, Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Default)]
pub enum GameFlow {
    /// The start screen: the `menu.bsn` document and its own camera.
    #[default]
    Menu,
    /// The playable world: `scene.bsn`, the player, the hud.
    Level,
    /// The end screen: the `gameover.bsn` document and its own camera.
    GameOver,
}

/// The boot mapping: the menu document boots as the menu, the
/// game-over document as the end screen, and any other start scene —
/// including the editor's own scene on a Play-Current launch — as a
/// world to play.
fn boot_state(start: &str) -> GameFlow {
    match start {
        MENU_SCENE => GameFlow::Menu,
        GAME_OVER_SCENE => GameFlow::GameOver,
        _ => GameFlow::Level,
    }
}

/// Marks the scene root the flow moves the worlds under. Three
/// documents pass through this root one at a time; the root entity and
/// its `Name` are the one stable thing across a switch.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct GameRoot;

/// The menu document's own marker, authored on its root in
/// `menu.bsn` — the hook anything that wants to find the menu looks
/// for (the editor's Play view, a BRP query, your own systems). The
/// `Default` here is load-bearing: the document's bare marker line
/// applies through `ReflectDefault`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct MenuRoot;

/// The game-over document's marker, same shape as [`MenuRoot`].
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct GameOverRoot;

/// Show the menu: re-bind the game root to the menu document, and
/// raise the UI camera that renders it, scoped to this state.
///
/// A switch is one handle insert: the runtime despawns the old
/// document's members and spawns the new one when it loads, so there is
/// nothing here to clean up by hand. And the very first `OnEnter` —
/// the boot state's — runs before `Startup` has spawned the root: there
/// is nothing to re-bind, and the root already boots on the document
/// the state was mapped from (they read the same `BootScene`).
fn enter_menu(
    mut commands: Commands,
    roots: Query<Entity, With<GameRoot>>,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    if let Ok(root) = roots.single() {
        commands
            .entity(root)
            .insert(JackdawSceneRoot(server.load(MENU_SCENE)));
    }
    // A UI document shown alone has no camera to render it (the level's
    // camera is the rig under the player), so each UI world brings its
    // own: 2D, marked as the UI camera, ordered above the rig's 3D one,
    // and removed with the state — `DespawnOnExit` is the whole
    // lifecycle.
    commands.spawn((
        Name::new("MenuUiCamera"),
        Camera2d,
        IsDefaultUiCamera,
        Camera {
            order: 1,
            ..default()
        },
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Menu),
    ));
}

/// Show the level: re-bind the game root to the level document. The
/// hud follows in the Update watcher below, the player and its rig
/// spawn once beside the root, and the level's props arrive with their
/// own `DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level)` — see the physics section.
fn enter_level(
    mut commands: Commands,
    roots: Query<Entity, With<GameRoot>>,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    if let Ok(root) = roots.single() {
        commands
            .entity(root)
            .insert(JackdawSceneRoot(server.load(LEVEL_SCENE)));
    }
}

/// Show the end screen: the game-over document and its camera, the
/// same shape as the menu.
fn enter_game_over(
    mut commands: Commands,
    roots: Query<Entity, With<GameRoot>>,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    if let Ok(root) = roots.single() {
        commands
            .entity(root)
            .insert(JackdawSceneRoot(server.load(GAME_OVER_SCENE)));
    }
    commands.spawn((
        Name::new("GameOverUiCamera"),
        Camera2d,
        IsDefaultUiCamera,
        Camera {
            order: 1,
            ..default()
        },
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::GameOver),
    ));
}

/// The level's HUD: spawned when the level becomes current, scoped to
/// it (`DespawnOnExit`), back fresh on the next entry. A watcher in
/// `Update` rather than an `OnEnter` spawn on purpose: the hud root
/// must land at a HIGHER entity index than the game root, or the
/// editor's Play would swap the hud document instead of the world —
/// and a boot into `Level` (Play-Current, or a flow file that names a
/// level) enters the state before `Startup` spawns the main root. This
/// way the ordering holds on every entry, not just the first.
fn spawn_hud_while_level(
    mut commands: Commands,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
    flow: Res<State<GameFlow>>,
    mut live: Local<bool>,
) {
    let live_now = flow.get() == &GameFlow::Level;
    if live_now && !*live {
        commands.spawn((
            JackdawSceneRoot(server.load(HUD_SCENE)),
            DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
        ));
    }
    *live = live_now;
}

/// Which screen is up inside the level. The hud's bindings read this
/// resource: the pause screen's `Visibility` is bound to the bool, so
/// flipping it is the whole show/hide mechanism. The worlds are
/// `GameFlow`'s to make; overlays inside one world are bools like this.
/// The other two fields are the save's display mirror (see
/// `mirror_save_display`): bindings resolve resources, so the menu and
/// game-over documents read the run counter here rather than naming
/// the generic save store in a document.
#[derive(Resource, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default)]
pub struct GameState {
    pub paused: bool,
    /// How many runs the save says have ended.
    pub runs_played: u32,
    /// The unlock line the menu shows; empty until `"endless"` is in
    /// the save's `unlocks`.
    pub unlock_note: String,
}

/// What a flow or hud button says. The button's `Action` binding in
/// `menu.bsn`, `hud.bsn` or `gameover.bsn` sends this with `button`
/// filled from its literal (`"start"`, `"resume"`, `"restart"`,
/// `"menu"`) — one event for every screen's buttons: the payload, not
/// the type, says what was pressed.
#[derive(Event, Reflect, Clone, Default)]
#[reflect(Event, Default)]
pub struct ScreenPressed {
    pub button: String,
}

/// The health the hud reads, live on the player. The component is the
/// run's state; `GameConfig.player_health` is the author's starting
/// number for it — `apply_health_from_config` below hands one to the
/// other. Same split as the falling cube's `speed` (component, live)
/// against the config's `move_speed` (asset, author intent).
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerHealth {
    pub current: f32,
    pub max: f32,
}

/// How much one hit takes off, wherever it comes from (the hazard
/// sensors with the physics module, the Interact key without).
const PLAYER_DAMAGE: f32 = 15.0;

/// Where a run begins — the first entry and every reset both use it,
/// so a restart lands exactly where the game started.
const PLAYER_SPAWN: Vec3 = Vec3::new(-2.0, 1.0, 0.0);

/// current/max -> 0..1 for the health bar's fill. Registered in
/// `GamePlugin` above: a binding's `via` can only call functions the
/// game has registered, and this one turns two component fields into
/// the one number a bar wants.
fn ratio(current: f32, max: f32) -> f32 {
    (current / max).clamp(0.0, 1.0)
}

/// Boolean not, for bindings that show a thing while a flag is false.
/// Registered like `ratio`.
fn not(value: bool) -> bool {
    !value
}

/// Zero means the run is over — registered like `ratio` (the
/// `is_zero`-bound veil in `hud_scenario` is the pattern; this game
/// ends its runs through `GameFlow` instead and keeps the function
/// here as the other half of the pair your own bindings reach for).
fn is_zero(value: f32) -> bool {
    value == 0.0
}

/// The marker the hud document itself carries, so the game can tell
/// the UI root from everything else it spawns. The `Default` here is
/// load-bearing: the document's bare `HudRoot` line applies through
/// `ReflectDefault`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct HudRoot;

/// Point the hud at the player: once the document spawns, its root
/// gets a `BindContext(player)` and every binding inside resolves the
/// player's components (the health below, the pickup counter the
/// physics module adds) against that entity. This insert is the whole
/// wiring — nothing else needs to know the UI exists. `Added` re-fires
/// for every fresh hud the flow spawns, so each entry into the level
/// re-wires itself.
fn wire_hud(
    mut commands: Commands,
    hud_roots: Query<Entity, Added<HudRoot>>,
    players: Query<Entity, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    let (Ok(root), Ok(player)) = (hud_roots.single(), players.single()) else {
        return;
    };
    commands.entity(root).insert(BindContext(player));
}

/// What the flow documents' buttons do. The bindings send
/// `ScreenPressed`; this observer is the whole behavior — Start and
/// Restart both move the flow to the level (Restart's reset is
/// `reset_run`, which runs on every entry into the level — switching
/// IS the reset), Menu goes back to the start screen, Resume drops the
/// pause veil. Every acknowledged press also sounds the button blip
/// (`press_sound` below).
fn on_screen_pressed(
    press: On<ScreenPressed>,
    mut flow: ResMut<NextState<GameFlow>>,
    mut state: ResMut<GameState>,
    mut commands: Commands,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    match press.event().button.as_str() {
        // Start and Restart both move the flow to the level: the run's
        // numbers come back through `reset_run` on the entry, so
        // Restart needs nothing of its own.
        "start" | "restart" => {
            press_sound(&mut commands, &server);
            flow.set(GameFlow::Level);
        }
        "menu" => {
            press_sound(&mut commands, &server);
            flow.set(GameFlow::Menu);
        }
        "resume" => {
            press_sound(&mut commands, &server);
            state.paused = false;
        }
        _ => {}
    }
}

/// The file the screen buttons acknowledge a press with.
const BLIP_SOUND: &str = "sounds/blip.wav";

/// One-shot feedback for a screen press: a short entity that plays
/// `blip.wav` on the sfx bus and takes itself down when the sound ends —
/// `PlaybackSettings::DESPAWN` is the whole lifecycle, and the entity's
/// brief life is the "stop" behavior a one-shot wants.
fn press_sound(commands: &mut Commands, server: &AssetServer) {
    // The turbofish is load-bearing: `AudioPlayer`'s generic has a default,
    // but inference will not apply it in an expression.
    commands.spawn((
        AudioPlayer::<AudioSource>(server.load(BLIP_SOUND)),
        PlaybackSettings::DESPAWN,
        SfxBus,
    ));
}

/// The run's numbers come back on every entry into the level — the
/// first one and every Restart both land here, so the end screen's
/// Restart button needs no reset logic of its own. Full health comes
/// straight from the config (the author's number, the same one
/// `apply_health_from_config` hands over on load), and the player goes
/// back to the spawn point. The player itself is not respawned: it
/// crossed the switch untouched (`spawn_player` runs once). Chained
/// right after `spawn_player` on purpose: a freshly spawned player
/// carries a zeroed health, and this is what fills it before any
/// game-over check can read it.
fn reset_run(
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut players: Query<(&mut PlayerHealth, &mut Transform), With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    for (mut health, mut transform) in &mut players {
        health.max = config.player_health;
        health.current = config.player_health;
        *transform = Transform::from_translation(PLAYER_SPAWN);
    }
}

/// The Pause action stops being a log line: Escape (or Start) flips
/// `paused` and the pause screen's visibility follows — and it belongs
/// to the level: on the menu or the end screen a screen already owns
/// the moment, so only a current `Level` answers. The Resume button
/// lands on the same bool through `on_screen_pressed` — one piece of
/// state, two doors in.
fn handle_pause_action(
    actions: Res<GameActions>,
    flow: Res<State<GameFlow>>,
    mut state: ResMut<GameState>,
) {
    if actions.just_pressed("Pause") && flow.get() == &GameFlow::Level {
        state.paused = !state.paused;
    }
}

/// The config hands its `player_health` to the component the run
/// actually reads: the config is the author's starting number, the
/// component is live state (damage moves it; the config does not
/// follow back). Runs on config change, so the file's first load and
/// an editor mid-run edit both carry — the same shape as gravity.
fn apply_health_from_config(
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut players: Query<&mut PlayerHealth, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if !config.is_changed() {
        return;
    }
    for mut health in &mut players {
        health.max = config.player_health;
        health.current = config.player_health;
    }
}

/// Damage lands here (the hazard sensors below, or the Interact key
/// when a game has no physics module). Clamped at zero; the game-over
/// check is what reacts.
fn take_damage(health: &mut PlayerHealth, amount: f32) {
    health.current = (health.current - amount).max(0.0);
}

/// Blood out ends the run: the flow moves to the game-over world, and
/// its `OnEnter` swaps the document and raises the end screen's
/// camera. Only a current `Level` can end — a player on the menu or
/// the end screen is not in a run.
fn update_game_over(
    flow: Res<State<GameFlow>>,
    mut next: ResMut<NextState<GameFlow>>,
    mut state: ResMut<GameState>,
    players: Query<&PlayerHealth, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if flow.get() != &GameFlow::Level {
        return;
    }
    for health in &players {
        if health.current <= 0.0 {
            state.paused = false;
            next.set(GameFlow::GameOver);
            break;
        }
    }
}
/// The player's velocity, in metres per second. `move_player` owns it
/// entirely — gravity adds to it, the ground and walls take from it,
/// a jump sets it — so it is runtime state, not an author knob (edit
/// `PlayerController` above to tune how the player moves).
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerVelocity(pub Vec3);

/// Everything the five actions do to the player, in one system:
///
/// * `Move` becomes a target horizontal velocity relative to the
///   camera (see `move_direction`);
/// * gravity — the same `GameConfig.gravity` the engine itself got
///   from `apply_gravity_from_config` — integrates onto the vertical
///   axis every frame, grounded or not;
/// * `Jump`, just-pressed and only when a short downward cast finds
///   ground that is not too steep, launches at `jump_speed`;
/// * `move_and_slide` then sweeps the capsule for the frame's
///   distance: as far as the way is clear, sliding along whatever is
///   in the way, and back out of anything already penetrated — that
///   sweep is the "collides with walls" half of a controller, and it
///   hands back the position and the leftover (slid) velocity.
///
/// None of it runs outside a run: the flow's `Level` state is the
/// world gate and `paused` the freeze inside it — the same two checks
/// the no-physics form makes, against the same state the hud reads.
fn move_player(
    actions: Res<GameActions>,
    flow: Res<State<GameFlow>>,
    state: Res<GameState>,
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    time: Res<Time>,
    move_and_slide: avian3d::prelude::MoveAndSlide,
    mut players: Query<
        (
            Entity,
            &PlayerController,
            &avian3d::prelude::Collider,
            &mut PlayerVelocity,
            &mut Transform,
        ),
        With<PlayerMarker>,
    >,
    look: Query<&CameraLook>,
) {
    if flow.get() != &GameFlow::Level || state.paused {
        return;
    }
    use avian3d::math::{ToF32Precision, ToRealPrecision};
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    let camera_yaw = look.single().map(|camera| camera.yaw).unwrap_or(0.0);
    let direction = move_direction(actions.value("Move"), camera_yaw);
    for (entity, controller, collider, mut velocity, mut transform) in &mut players {
        velocity.0.x = direction.x * controller.move_speed;
        velocity.0.z = direction.z * controller.move_speed;
        velocity.0.y += config.gravity * dt;
        // Exclude the player's own collider from its queries, or the
        // first sweep hits the player.
        let filter =
            avian3d::prelude::SpatialQueryFilter::from_excluded_entities([entity]);
        // A short cast straight down from the resting capsule finds the
        // ground (a hit whose surface is not too steep); skin width
        // keeps the resting capsule a hair above it, so the 0.1 m cast
        // is enough.
        let grounded = move_and_slide
            .spatial_query
            .cast_shape(
                collider,
                transform.translation.real(),
                transform.rotation,
                Dir3::NEG_Y,
                &avian3d::prelude::ShapeCastConfig::from_max_distance(0.1),
                &filter,
            )
            .is_some_and(|hit| hit.normal1.y > 0.7);
        if grounded && actions.just_pressed("Jump") {
            velocity.0.y = controller.jump_speed;
        }
        let output = move_and_slide.move_and_slide(
            collider,
            transform.translation.real(),
            transform.rotation,
            velocity.0.real(),
            time.delta(),
            &avian3d::prelude::MoveAndSlideConfig::default(),
            &filter,
            // Per-hit handling (footsteps, events) would go here; the
            // default accepts every hit and slides.
            |_| avian3d::prelude::MoveAndSlideHitResponse::Accept,
        );
        transform.translation = output.position.f32();
        velocity.0 = output.projected_velocity;
        face_movement_direction(direction, dt, &mut transform);
    }
    if actions.just_pressed("Interact") {
        info!("Interact!");
    }
}
// ---- Physics & triggers: the pickup demo -------------------------
// The stage, the pickups and the systems above them. Everything a
// game needs to copy: spawn a `Sensor` collider, read `TriggerEvents`.
// Every prop below is level scenery — spawned on entering the level,
// `DespawnOnExit` takes it when the level ends, and the next entry
// starts from a fresh set.

/// A thing to walk over and collect: a small glowing sensor cube.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct Pickup;

/// How many pickups the player holds, carried by the player entity
/// (a component, so the editor's Play view and an exported build can
/// both see the count move).
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PickupCount(pub u32);

/// The floor everything stands on: one big static collider.
fn spawn_stage(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    commands.spawn((
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(20.0, 1.0, 20.0))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.15, 0.15, 0.18),
            ..default()
        })),
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, -0.5, 0.0),
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
        jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
            avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Cuboid {
                x_length: 20.0,
                y_length: 1.0,
                z_length: 20.0,
            },
        ),
    ));
}

/// Three pickups in a line between the player and the falling cube.
/// (`reset_run_counters` puts the count back when the level starts
/// over; the props themselves come back through `DespawnOnExit` and
/// the level-entry spawns.)
fn spawn_pickups(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    spawn_pickup_line(&mut commands, &mut meshes, &mut materials);
}

/// The pickup cubes themselves, one line of three. Its own function so
/// a restart lays the line down again after the run despawned it.
fn spawn_pickup_line(
    commands: &mut Commands,
    meshes: &mut Assets<Mesh>,
    materials: &mut Assets<StandardMaterial>,
) {
    for i in 0..3 {
        commands.spawn((
            Pickup,
            Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(0.3, 0.3, 0.3))),
            MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
                base_color: Color::srgb(0.1, 0.7, 0.2),
                emissive: LinearRgba::rgb(0.1, 1.6, 0.3),
                ..default()
            })),
            Transform::from_xyz(-1.0 + i as f32, 0.5, 0.0),
            DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
            jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
                avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Sphere {
                    radius: 0.35,
                },
            ),
            // The trigger: detects, does not block. The bridge adds
            // the event prerequisite avian asks for.
            avian3d::prelude::Sensor,
        ));
    }
}

/// Two walls at right angles on the stage: something solid to walk
/// into. `move_player`'s sweep does the rest — walk at the north wall
/// (ahead of the spawn) head-on and stop; walk at it diagonally and
/// slide along it. Static, like the stage: a collider with no rigid
/// body stands still.
fn spawn_walls(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    let wall = materials.add(StandardMaterial {
        base_color: Color::srgb(0.45, 0.4, 0.35),
        ..default()
    });
    for (x, z, x_length, z_length) in [(0.0, -3.0, 12.0, 0.4), (-4.0, 0.0, 0.4, 8.0)] {
        commands.spawn((
            Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(x_length, 1.5, z_length))),
            MeshMaterial3d(wall.clone()),
            Transform::from_xyz(x, 0.75, z),
            DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
            jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
                avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Cuboid {
                    x_length,
                    y_length: 1.5,
                    z_length,
                },
            ),
        ));
    }
}

/// The pickup pattern itself: when the *player* starts touching a
/// `Pickup`, the pickup goes away and the count goes up; the third
/// one wins the run — the flow moves to the game-over screen, the
/// very move blood-out makes, so the end screen and its Restart are
/// the win's way out too. The other side of the contact is checked
/// on purpose — a prop merely settling onto a pickup (the falling
/// cube landing on one) must not collect it. Swap `Pickup` for your
/// own marker and this is a coin, a key, a landmine — whatever a
/// walk-over should do.
fn collect_pickups(
    triggers: Res<TriggerEvents>,
    pickups: Query<(), With<Pickup>>,
    mut players: Query<&mut PickupCount, With<PlayerMarker>>,
    mut flow: ResMut<NextState<GameFlow>>,
    mut commands: Commands,
    server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    let mut collected = 0u32;
    for contact in triggers.enters() {
        if pickups.contains(contact.trigger) && players.contains(contact.other) {
            commands.entity(contact.trigger).despawn();
            collected += 1;
        }
    }
    if collected == 0 {
        return;
    }
    let Ok(mut count) = players.single_mut() else {
        return;
    };
    count.0 += collected;
    if count.0 >= 3 {
        info!("Level clear! All {} pickups collected.", count.0);
        // The win acknowledges itself with the button blip: the same
        // one-shot `press_sound` sends, riding the sfx bus and taking
        // itself down when it ends.
        commands.spawn((
            AudioPlayer::<AudioSource>(server.load(BLIP_SOUND)),
            PlaybackSettings::DESPAWN,
            SfxBus,
        ));
        flow.set(GameFlow::GameOver);
    }
}

/// Hand the config's gravity number to the engine: `GameConfig` is
/// the saved author's truth, the engine's `Gravity` resource is what
/// the simulation reads. Runs whenever the config changes, so a
/// re-load mid-run (the editor's config panel) carries over too.
fn apply_gravity_from_config(
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut gravity: ResMut<avian3d::prelude::Gravity>,
) {
    if config.is_changed() {
        gravity.0 = Vec3::new(0.0, config.gravity, 0.0);
    }
}

/// A thing that hurts to touch: a glowing red sensor on the walk
/// line. Carries the collision-layer row the docs' table gives
/// hazards (member of layer 3, filtered against layer 1), so its
/// contacts exist with the player and nothing else — the pickups and
/// walls it passes never feel it.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct Hazard;

fn spawn_hazards(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    commands.spawn((
        Hazard,
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(0.4, 0.4, 0.4))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.8, 0.1, 0.1),
            emissive: LinearRgba::rgb(1.2, 0.1, 0.1),
            ..default()
        })),
        Transform::from_xyz(2.0, 0.5, 0.0),
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
        jackdaw_avian_integration::AvianCollider(
            avian3d::prelude::ColliderConstructor::Sphere {
                radius: 0.35,
            },
        ),
        avian3d::prelude::Sensor,
        // The docs' hazard row, live: `memberships 0b100, filters
        // 0b001` — hazard hits the player only.
        avian3d::prelude::CollisionLayers::new(
            avian3d::prelude::LayerMask(0b100),
            avian3d::prelude::LayerMask(0b001),
        ),
    ));
}

/// The hazard pattern, the other half of `collect_pickups`: when the
/// *player* starts touching a `Hazard`, health drops (`take_damage`,
/// in the game-flow section above) and the hud's bar follows the
/// component. `enters()` fires when the overlap starts, not every
/// frame — walk out and back in to take it again. Nothing fires outside
/// a run: the same `GameFlow`/`paused` gate as the movement system.
fn hazard_damage(
    triggers: Res<TriggerEvents>,
    flow: Res<State<GameFlow>>,
    state: Res<GameState>,
    hazards: Query<(), With<Hazard>>,
    mut players: Query<&mut PlayerHealth, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if flow.get() != &GameFlow::Level || state.paused {
        return;
    }
    for contact in triggers.enters() {
        if hazards.contains(contact.trigger) && players.contains(contact.other) {
            if let Ok(mut health) = players.single_mut() {
                take_damage(&mut health, PLAYER_DAMAGE);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The counter half of a restart: the level entry despawned nothing of
/// the player's — the player crossed the switch — so the run's numbers
/// are put back here instead. (The health and the spawn point are the
/// game-flow section's `reset_run`; this is the pickup-and-velocity
/// half, which only exists with the physics module.) Runs on every
/// entry into the level, so Restart is just a switch to `Level`.
fn reset_run_counters(
    mut players: Query<(&mut PickupCount, &mut PlayerVelocity), With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    for (mut count, mut velocity) in &mut players {
        count.0 = 0;
        velocity.0 = Vec3::ZERO;
    }
}
